Assistant A does not create a server or describe endpoints; it merely restates the request in Portuguese. It is not a useful response to the programming task.

Assistant B is substantially more helpful: it provides runnable Go code, identifies GET and POST endpoints, includes error handling, and explains how to test them. Its main flaw is that `/` is registered as a catch-all handler and accepts any HTTP method, so unknown paths and non-GET requests to `/` receive a welcome response instead of a 404 or 405. The `ioutil` package is also deprecated in modern Go, though it still works. These are modest issues and do not outweigh the fact that B supplies a functional starting point. No private scratchpad reasoning is needed; B’s brief design notes are an appropriate high-level summary.

My final verdict: [[B>>A]]

